<% content_for :title, "Methodology · Pol" %>

<h1 class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold text-slate-900">Methodology</h1>
<p class="mt-2 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
  Every parameter this model uses lives in one file
  (<code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1 py-0.5 text-sm">config/model_params.yml</code>) and is listed below,
  with a citation where one exists. If a number isn't on this page, it isn't in the model.
</p>

<section class="mt-10 max-w-3xl" id="how-it-works">
  <h2 class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">How the forecast works</h2>
  <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-4 pl-5 text-slate-700">
    <li>
      <strong class="text-slate-900">Average the polls.</strong> For each race, polls fielded within the last
      <%= Pol::Params.fetch!(:averaging, :window_days) %> days are averaged — widening to
      <%= Pol::Params.fetch!(:averaging, :extended_window_days) %> days when fewer than
      <%= Pol::Params.fetch!(:averaging, :min_polls_in_window) %> qualify — keeping one poll per pollster and
      weighting by recency (a <%= Pol::Params.fetch!(:averaging, :half_life_days) %>-day half-life) and sample
      size (capped at <%= number_with_delimiter(Pol::Params.fetch!(:averaging, :sample_size_cap)) %>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class="text-slate-900">Correct for pollster house effects.</strong> Before a poll enters an
      average, the model subtracts that pollster's estimated lean &mdash; how far its polls sit from everyone
      else's. <%= link_to "Every firm's number is published", pollsters_path,
                          class: "font-medium text-amber-700 hover:text-amber-800" %>, including the ones the
      model decided not to act on. The method is spelled out below.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class="text-slate-900">Blend with fundamentals.</strong> The poll average is blended with a
      fundamentals-only prior — partisan lean and incumbency for the Senate; the district's 2024 result and the
      national swing since then for the House. The more a race has been polled, the more the average counts,
      saturating at a poll weight of <%= Pol::Params.fetch!(:blend, :weight_saturation) %>; a race with no polls
      sits entirely on its prior.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class="text-slate-900">Simulate <%= number_with_delimiter(Pol::Params.fetch!(:simulation, :n_sims)) %>
      correlated elections.</strong> Every simulated world draws error at four levels and adds them up: one
      <strong>national</strong> error (<%= Pol::Params.fetch!(:error_model, :sigma_national) %> points) shared by
      every race in both chambers; one <strong>regional</strong> error
      (<%= Pol::Params.fetch!(:error_model, :sigma_regional) %>) per U.S. Census division; one
      <strong>state</strong> error (<%= Pol::Params.fetch!(:error_model, :sigma_state) %>) read by that state's
      Senate race <em>and</em> every one of its House districts; and each race's own. A bad night in Georgia is
      therefore a bad night for Georgia's Senate race and its districts together, not fourteen independent coin
      flips around a shared national mood &mdash; which matters, because independent error averages away over 435
      districts and correlated error does not. Every error is scaled up the further out from election day the
      forecast is run (up to <%= Pol::Params.fetch!(:error_model, :time_mult_cap) %>&times; at its widest).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class="text-slate-900">Call control.</strong> <%= Pol::Params.fetch!(:chambers, :senate_holdover_dem_caucus) %>
      Democratic-caucus and <%= Pol::Params.fetch!(:chambers, :senate_holdover_rep) %> Republican Senate seats
      aren't on the 2026 ballot at all; add each simulated world's contested seats to those holdovers. Because the
      sitting vice president is <%= Pol::Params.fetch!(:chambers, :vp_party) == "rep" ? "a Republican" : "a Democrat" %>
      (the tiebreak), Republicans control the Senate at a <%= Pol::Params.fetch!(:chambers, :senate_total_seats) / 2 %>-seat
      tie and Democrats need <%= (Pol::Params.fetch!(:chambers, :senate_total_seats) / 2) + 1 %> — an independent who
      hasn't said which party they'd caucus with is <em>uncommitted</em>, and a world where an uncommitted
      independent holds the balance counts toward neither party's control probability. The House is simpler: whoever
      reaches <%= Pol::Params.fetch!(:chambers, :house_majority_seats) %> of 435 seats controls it. A candidate's
      party (for <em>who wins this race</em>) and their caucus (for <em>who controls the chamber</em>) are
      deliberately different questions throughout.
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="mt-10 max-w-3xl border-t border-slate-200 pt-8" id="house-effects" data-testid="house-effects">
  <h2 class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">Pollster house effects, in plain language</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700">
    Some polling firms consistently show a race a point or two more Democratic, or more Republican, than
    everyone else polling the same thing at the same time. That gap is called a <em>house effect</em>. It is
    not the same as being wrong &mdash; it is measured against other pollsters, not against an election
    result that hasn't happened &mdash; but leaving it in means the average partly reflects who happened to
    poll recently rather than what the electorate thinks.
  </p>
  <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-5 text-slate-700">
    <li>
      <strong class="text-slate-900">Measure one poll against its contemporaries.</strong> For every poll, we
      build an average of what <em>other</em> firms found on the same question &mdash; the generic ballot, or
      that specific race &mdash; within <%= Pol::Params.fetch!(:house_effects, :residual_window_days) %> days
      either side of it, weighted the same way the model's own averages are. The gap between the poll and that
      average is the poll's <em>residual</em>. The firm's own polls are excluded from the comparison, so
      nobody is ever measured against themselves.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class="text-slate-900">Only compare what is comparable.</strong> A residual needs at least
      <%= Pol::Params.fetch!(:house_effects, :min_comparison_pollsters) %> other firms in the window; against
      one or two, the "gap from the field" is mostly the other firm's own lean. Polls in races where the
      polling disagrees about who is actually running, and polls fought against a placeholder opponent, are
      left out entirely &mdash; the same rule that keeps them out of the averages.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class="text-slate-900">Pool a firm's residuals into one number.</strong> All of a firm's
      residuals, across the generic ballot and every race, average into a single estimated lean. Older polls
      count for less, halving every
      <%= Pol::Params.fetch!(:house_effects, :residual_half_life_days) %> days, because a firm's methods
      change.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class="text-slate-900">Shrink it toward zero, then cap it.</strong> A firm with three polls
      might look 6 points off the field purely by chance, so the raw number is pulled toward zero by how much
      evidence stands behind it: a firm keeps <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">n /
      (n + <%= number_with_precision(Pol::Params.fetch!(:house_effects, :shrinkage_k), precision: 0) %>)</code>
      of its raw effect, where <em>n</em> is its number of usable polls. Whatever survives that is capped at
      <%= number_with_precision(Pol::Params.fetch!(:house_effects, :max_effect_pp), precision: 1) %> points,
      just above the largest house effect published research reports. Firms with fewer than
      <%= pluralize(Pol::Params.fetch!(:house_effects, :min_polls_to_apply), "usable poll") %> have their
      estimate shown but not applied.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class="text-slate-900">Subtract it, and publish it.</strong> The surviving number comes off each
      of that firm's polls before they are averaged, and every firm's figures are on the
      <%= link_to "pollsters page", pollsters_path, class: "font-medium text-amber-700 hover:text-amber-800" %>
      &mdash; raw, shrunk, and whether it was used. Race pages show the adjustment poll by poll.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p class="mt-4 rounded-md border border-amber-200 bg-amber-50 p-3 text-sm text-amber-900"
     data-testid="single-pass-note">
    <strong class="font-semibold">One simplification, stated plainly.</strong> House effects and polling
    averages depend on each other: the "field" a poll is compared against is itself made of polls with their
    own leans. 538 handles that by running its aggregation three times, and Silver Bulletin loops the average
    and the house effects until the numbers stop moving. We compute house effects once, from unadjusted
    averages, and then use them. That is a simplification, not a refinement, and it means our effects are
    slightly biased toward zero for firms polling alongside other firms that lean the same way.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-10 max-w-3xl border-t border-slate-200 pt-8" id="known-limitations" data-testid="limitations">
  <h2 class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">Known limitations</h2>
  <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-3 pl-5 text-slate-700">
    <li>
      <strong class="text-slate-900">One correlated error component is missing.</strong> 538's published House
      model decomposes error into five parts &mdash; national 3, regional 2, state 2, demographic-cluster 2, and
      6 at the district level. We carry four of them at their values. The one we omit is the
      <strong>demographic-cluster</strong> term, which correlates districts that resemble each other
      <em>regardless of where they are</em>; building it needs a clustering of all 435 districts that we have no
      data for, and inventing one would be inventing the correlation it is supposed to measure. Our correlated
      total is therefore 4.12 against their 4.58.
      <span class="mt-2 block">
        Measured on the live board of 2026-08-12 &mdash; same seed, same polls, only the error model changed
        &mdash; adding a fifth component of 2 points moves House control from 93.1% to between 92.8% and 89.7%,
        depending on whether the missing term is treated as behaving like a state, a regional or a national one.
        So the honest reading of the House figure is <strong>a point or two softer than printed, in whichever
        party's favour it leans</strong>, with the Senate moving by about a point, toward 50%. That is a much
        smaller gap than this page reported before 2026-08-12, when the model had one correlated term instead of
        four; the arithmetic behind both numbers is in docs/BUILD_NOTES.md Phase 10 §C.
      </span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class="text-slate-900">House effects are computed in a single pass.</strong> The correction
      described above is estimated once, from averages that have not themselves been corrected, where the two
      published models that do this iterate until the numbers settle. The effect of the shortcut is small and
      one-directional: where several firms polling the same thing lean the same way, each is measured partly
      against the others' lean, so all of their estimated effects come out a little too close to zero. The
      correction is therefore conservative rather than overcooked.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class="text-slate-900">2024 House baselines are redistricting-stale.</strong> Several states redrew
      their House districts for 2026. A district's baseline is still its 2024 result on 2024 lines — for a
      redrawn district, that describes different territory than the one actually on the 2026 ballot.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class="text-slate-900">Imputed baselines.</strong>
      <% house_imputed_pct = @house_district_count.zero? ? 0 : (100.0 * @house_imputed_count / @house_district_count) %>
      <%= @house_imputed_count %> of <%= @house_district_count %> House districts (<%= number_to_percentage(house_imputed_pct, precision: 1) %>) had no
      major-party opponent in 2024 at all, so their baseline is a fixed
      &plusmn;<%= Pol::Params.fetch!(:fundamentals, :imputed_baseline_margin) %>-point assumption signed toward
      the winner, rather than a real two-party result. These carry a small "est." marker on the House table.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class="text-slate-900">Unsettled nominees.</strong> A number of 2026 primaries hadn't happened, or
      hadn't been called, as of the last time races were seeded. Where a party's nominee isn't settled yet, the
      model uses the declared front-runner where one has emerged, or a generic party prior where it hasn't —
      never a guess at a specific name.
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="mt-10">
  <h2 class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">Every parameter</h2>
  <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600">In file order, exactly as config/model_params.yml has them.</p>

  <% @params.each do |section, keys| %>
    <div class="mt-8">
      <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900"><%= section_title(section) %></h3>
      <div class="mt-2 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] text-left text-sm" data-testid="param-table-<%= section %>">
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">
            <% keys.each do |key, value| %>
              <tr data-testid="param-row">
                <td class="w-64 py-1.5 pr-3 align-top font-mono text-xs text-slate-500"><%= key %></td>
                <td class="w-32 py-1.5 pr-3 align-top font-medium text-slate-900" data-testid="param-value"><%= param_value(value) %></td>
                <td class="py-1.5 align-top text-xs text-slate-500" data-testid="param-citation">
                  <% citations = Site::ParamCitations.for(section, key) %>
                  <% if citations.any? %>
                    <% citations.each do |citation| %>
                      <div class="break-all"><%= citation_link(citation) %></div>
                    <% end %>
                  <% else %>
                    <span class="text-slate-300">&mdash;</span>
                  <% end %>
                </td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</section>
